WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.490
Local models should be evaluated against real workflow needs.

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:08.386
A model that sounds fluent may still miss instructions, invent details, ignore formatting,

00:00:08.386 --> 00:00:11.022
overstate certainty, or fail on domain-specific language.

00:00:11.022 --> 00:00:14.036
Evaluation turns model selection from guesswork into evidence.

00:00:14.036 --> 00:00:17.049
Repeatable prompt sets are a practical starting point.

00:00:17.049 --> 00:00:21.568
Create representative prompts for the work the model will actually support: drafting,

00:00:21.568 --> 00:00:24.205
summarization, extraction, coding assistance, comparison, or review.

00:00:24.205 --> 00:00:28.348
Run the same prompts across candidate models and note what changes.

00:00:28.348 --> 00:00:32.491
Quality is more than whether the output is pleasant to read.

00:00:32.491 --> 00:00:35.504
Track hallucinations, missing constraints, formatting problems, unsafe assumptions,

00:00:35.504 --> 00:00:37.764
refusal behavior where appropriate, latency, stability,

00:00:37.764 --> 00:00:41.153
and how often a human must repair the result.

00:00:41.153 --> 00:00:45.673
These observations help the team choose a model that fits the workflow.

00:00:45.673 --> 00:00:47.933
Acceptance criteria should match task risk.

00:00:47.933 --> 00:00:53.206
A brainstorming assistant can tolerate more uncertainty than a workflow that influences customer communication,

00:00:53.206 --> 00:00:55.842
compliance review, security review, or operational decisions.

00:00:55.842 --> 00:00:59.609
Higher-risk workflows need stronger human review and clearer quality gates.

00:00:59.609 --> 00:01:00.739
Document the findings.

00:01:00.739 --> 00:01:06.012
Record which model was tested, what prompts were used, what settings mattered, what failed,

00:01:06.012 --> 00:01:09.025
and what the model is approved to do.

00:01:09.025 --> 00:01:12.791
Good evaluation makes local AI workflows easier to defend, improve,

00:01:12.791 --> 00:01:15.427
and retire when they no longer fit.

00:01:15.427 --> 00:01:19.947
It also helps users avoid repeating the same model comparison from scratch.

00:01:19.947 --> 00:01:25.220
Evaluation notes should be plain enough that another maintainer can understand the decision later.
